  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe primary approach involves using a particle swarm optimization (PSO) algorithm to explore the search space more efficiently than traditional gradient-based methods. This addresses the bottleneck of local optima by leveraging population diversity and dynamic adaptation. By initializing the swarm with a more distributed configuration and incorporating adaptive mutation strategies, the algorithm can better balance exploration and exploitation. The PSO-based approach allows for more flexible search, while maintaining the integral constraint through rescaling mechanisms.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe representation uses a step function h(x) with n_points samples on [0, 2], satisfying:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2\n\nThe objective is to minimize:\nC\u2085 = max_k [\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx] * dx\n\nWhere dx = 2.0 / n_points. Constraints are enforced via:\n- A hard constraint \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2\n- Bounds 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n\nThe optimization uses a hybrid of PSO with adaptive inertia weight and L-BFGS-B refinement. Each particle represents a candidate solution, and the swarm dynamics evolve based on personal and global best solutions. The social and cognitive components guide the particle search, while the inertial weight balances exploration and exploitation. The integral constraint is maintained through post-optimization rescaling after clipping and repair.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Input: n_points = 400, initial_h_values (if available)\nb) Initialize swarm:\n   - If initial_h_values exists, use it as base\n   - Else, generate 20 particles with randomized spreading patterns:\n      i) Uniformly distribute selected positions, then interpolate\n      ii) Apply exponential decay to ensure mass distribution \n      iii) Scale to meet target sum\nc) PSO loop:\n   - Iterate for 200 generations:\n      i) For each particle, evaluate objective C\u2085\n      ii) Update personal and global best positions\n      iii) Adjust inertial weight (0.5 to 0.9 over time)\n      iv) Apply random mutations with amplitude 0.02-0.05\n      v) Project to feasibility: clip values and rescale\nd) Track best solution via:\n   - Maintain top 5% of particles as elites\n   - Evaluate objective after each iteration\n   - Retain solution with lowest C\u2085\ne) Final refinement:\n   - Apply L-BFGS-B for 50 iterations on top elite\n   - Use hierarchical constraints with penalty terms\nf) Output: best_h.tolist(), best_c5, n_points\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_particles = 20\n- Mutation amplitude = 0.025-0.05\n- Inertial weight = 0.5-0.9 (linear decay)\n- Generations = 200\n- Refinement steps = 50\n- Elite fraction = 5%\n\nVariations:\n- Swarm size: 10, 20, 30\n- Mutation range: 0.01-0.1\n- Inertial weight: static vs dynamic\n- Elite replacement strategy\n- Randomized initialization vs gradient steps\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- 70% for PSO exploration\n- 20% for L-BFGS refinement\n- 10% for constraint handling\n\nTermination checks:\n- After 200 iterations (60s)\n- If worst solution stagnates for 30 iterations\n\nFallback:\n- If parent exists, use it as baseline\n- If no valid candidate, return parent with penalty\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nChecks:\n- All h[i] \u2208 [0,1] (via clipping)\n- \u2211h = n_points / 2 (via rescaling)\n- Objective evaluation matches np.correlate with full mode\n\nRe-evaluation:\n- Final candidate re-evaluated with exact evaluator\n- Sanity check: max lag overlap \u2264 0.38085\n- Validate feasibility with sum check\n- Confirm bounds using np.all((h >= 0) & (h <= 1))",
